 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is significantly faster than NeRFs, often completing in under an hour for complex scenes [S5, unofficial]. The C++ implementation with Metal backend achieves training in ~90 seconds on an M4 Max [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. Default max is 5,000,000 [S3, unofficial]. Sinc splatting variants consume less memory than standard Gaussians [S7].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves comparable image quality to NeRFs with real-time rendering speeds [S2]. On the Replica dataset, SGS-SLAM achieves PSNR 34.15, SSIM 0.97, and LPIPS 0.096 [S1].
-*   **Hardware:** Supports CUDA, MPS, and CPU. CPU execution is ~100x slower than GPU [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast training, real-time rendering, high fidelity.
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to initialization; prone to "floaters" in unconstrained settings without regularization [S2][S6, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically takes 15-30 minutes for standard scenes (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360) on modern GPUs, significantly faster than NeRFs which can take hours [S5, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians. Default max is 5,000,000 Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. Sinc splatting variants consume less memory than standard Gaussians [S7].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the Replica dataset, SGS-SLAM achieves PSNR 34.15, SSIM 0.97, and LPIPS 0.096 [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance degrades on CPU, which is ~100x slower than GPU [S3, unofficial]. Resolution scaling (downscaling) is used during early training to accelerate convergence [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, fast training, high fidelity [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can produce "floaters" or artifacts in dynamic scenes or unconstrained settings without modifications (e.g., WildGaussians) [S3, unofficial]. Sensitive to initialization and local minima in some variants [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced as a method for real-time radiance field rendering [9]. The original method initializes positions using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [21, S2]. It differs from NeRFs by using explicit Gaussian primitives instead of implicit neural networks, enabling faster training and rendering [S2][S5, unofficial]. The training process relies on adaptive density control to manage the number and size of Gaussians [S2].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. [9] (cited in [S2][S6, unofficial]). It initializes positions using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [S2][S6, unofficial]. The method falls under the category of radiance fields but differs from NeRFs by using explicit Gaussian primitives [S5, unofficial]. It has been widely adopted for SLAM [S1], dynamic scene reconstruction [S1], and human avatars [S1].
 
